WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.214
The consul looked at Fix in surprise and said, "There’s a thief on board the Mongolia ? Are you

2
00:00:08.215 --> 00:00:15.106
certain?" "Consul, I have no doubt. I have spotted my man," said Fix with

3
00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:22.533
conviction. "He passes himself off as an eccentric, rich man who is going around the world in eighty

4
00:00:22.534 --> 00:00:27.843
days. When he arrives to have his passport stamped, you must hold him

5
00:00:27.844 --> 00:00:33.370
here!" "He is coming to have a visa entered in his passport?" asked the

6
00:00:33.371 --> 00:00:38.807
consul. "Yes, but I hope you will not issue the visa," said

7
00:00:38.808 --> 00:00:43.453
Fix. "Why not? If the passport is genuine, I have no right to refuse."

8
00:00:43.454 --> 00:00:48.217
"Criminals such as this man use passports to flee the law.

9
00:00:48.218 --> 00:00:53.172
You must keep him here!" replied Fix, his frustration growing.

10
00:00:53.173 --> 00:00:58.592
"I cannot hold a man, no matter how sure you are of his guilt, without a warrant.

11
00:00:58.593 --> 00:01:02.658
Surely you are aware of that," replied the consul, shaking his head.

12
00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:07.724
"Perhaps this Mr. Fogg won’t even come here if he is indeed the person you suppose him to be.

13
00:01:07.725 --> 00:01:11.886
A robber doesn’t like to leave traces of his flight behind him, you know.

14
00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:16.661
Besides, he is not obligated to have his passport stamped anyway."

15
00:01:16.662 --> 00:01:22.418
"If he is as shrewd as I think he is, consul, he will—" began Fix.

16
00:01:22.419 --> 00:01:26.621
But before he could finish, someone knocked on the door.

17
00:01:26.622 --> 00:01:32.986
Fix quickly hid himself in a corner of the room as Passepartout and his master entered the office.

18
00:01:32.987 --> 00:01:39.763
From his hiding place, Fix observed as the consul took Mr. Fogg’s documents and read them carefully.

19
00:01:39.764 --> 00:01:44.474
"You are Mr. Phileas Fogg?" asked the consul.

20
00:01:44.475 --> 00:01:46.633
"I am," said Fogg.

21
00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:50.811
"And this is your servant?" asked the consul. "He

22
00:01:50.812 --> 00:01:55.407
is," said Fogg. "A Frenchman named Passepartout." "You

23
00:01:55.408 --> 00:01:58.188
are from London?" asked the consul. "Yes,"

24
00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:00.071
said Fogg. "And

25
00:02:00.072 --> 00:02:03.462
you are going . . . ?" asked the consul. "To

26
00:02:03.463 --> 00:02:06.372
Bombay," replied Mr. Fogg. "Very

27
00:02:06.373 --> 00:02:13.514
good, sir," said the consul. "You know that no passport is required and a visa would be meaningless?" "I

28
00:02:13.515 --> 00:02:16.620
know it, sir," replied Phileas Fogg. "But

29
00:02:16.621 --> 00:02:21.608
I wish to prove, by your visa, that I traveled via Suez." "Very

30
00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:29.024
well, sir." The consul proceeded to sign and date the passport, and then added his official seal. Mr.

31
00:02:29.025 --> 00:02:34.642
Fogg paid the fee, bowed, and went out, followed by Passepartout. After

32
00:02:34.643 --> 00:02:42.144
leaving the consul’s office, Mr. Fogg returned to the dock and gave some instructions to Passepartout. Then

33
00:02:42.145 --> 00:02:47.426
he made his way to his cabin on the Mongolia and picked up his travel journal. In

34
00:02:47.427 --> 00:02:53.338
this journal a detailed itinerary listed all the major stopping points on Mr. Fogg’s route: Paris,

35
00:02:53.339 --> 00:03:04.176
Brindisi, Suez, Bombay, Calcutta, Singapore, Hong Kong, Yokohama, San Francisco, New York, and finally London. Next

36
00:03:04.177 --> 00:03:12.180
to each was written the expected day and time of arrival with a space for noting the actual arrival time and the hours gained or lost. As a

37
00:03:12.181 --> 00:03:17.786
result Mr. Fogg always knew whether he was behind or in advance of his schedule. He added

38
00:03:17.787 --> 00:03:24.359
his newest entry: Reached Suez Wednesday, October 9, at 11:00 a.m. Total of

39
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.135
hours spent: 158; or in days: six and a half. As Fogg

40
00:03:30.136 --> 00:03:36.536
closed his journal and put away his pen, he observed that he had neither gained nor lost time. He sat

41
00:03:36.537 --> 00:03:43.001
down quietly to eat breakfast in his cabin, never once thinking of visiting the town. After seeing

42
00:03:43.002 --> 00:03:48.650
Mr. Fogg in person, Fix was convinced that Fogg was the culprit. Fogg fit

43
00:03:48.651 --> 00:03:52.236
the description of the robber perfectly. "Everything adds

44
00:03:52.237 --> 00:03:56.347
up," Fix said to himself. "His leaving

45
00:03:56.348 --> 00:04:04.877
London so quickly, his hiring a new servant the very same day . . . It’s all very suspicious." Fix quickly

46
00:04:04.878 --> 00:04:08.293
decided that he must follow Mr. Fogg. It was

47
00:04:08.294 --> 00:04:18.547
his duty, after all, and the reward for catching such a cunning criminal as this supposed gentleman would be his. Fix turned to the consul. "I must leave

48
00:04:18.548 --> 00:04:22.985
at once. I am going to telegraph the police in London. I must follow

49
00:04:22.986 --> 00:04:26.490
this man!" he said. The detective thanked

50
00:04:26.491 --> 00:04:30.405
the consul for his help and left. Fix went immediately

51
00:04:30.406 --> 00:04:36.831
to the telegraph office and sent the following wire: Suez to London. Rowan, Commissioner of

52
00:04:36.832 --> 00:04:42.824
Police, Scotland Yard. I’ve found the bank robber, Phileas Fogg. Send an arrest

53
00:04:42.825 --> 00:04:48.144
warrant to Bombay without delay. Fix, Detective. A quarter of

54
00:04:48.145 --> 00:04:56.611
an hour later, with a small bag in his hand, Fix boarded the Mongolia . Passepartout's Guidebook

55
00:04:56.612 --> 00:05:00.747
During our journey it's my job to keep track of Mr. Fogg’s passport.

56
00:05:00.748 --> 00:05:04.144
If I lose it, I will be in big trouble!

57
00:05:04.145 --> 00:05:08.356
Whenever you travel abroad, you must bring a passport with you.

58
00:05:08.357 --> 00:05:13.597
A passport is a document issued by your country for the purpose of overseas travel.

59
00:05:13.598 --> 00:05:19.499
The word "passport" most likely comes from the medieval word for the gate, or porte , of a

60
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.048
city. Notice my name, Passepartout, looks a lot like the word

61
00:05:24.049 --> 00:05:27.248
"passport." Hmm . . . that's a funny coincidence, isn't

62
00:05:27.249 --> 00:05:33.306
it! Foreign governments use passports to identify who is arriving in, or leaving, their country.

63
00:05:33.307 --> 00:05:39.207
When you arrive in a foreign country, you must obtain a visa, which gives you permission to enter the country.

64
00:05:39.208 --> 00:05:43.665
The visa is either stamped into or attached to your passport.

65
00:05:43.666 --> 00:05:50.225
I have to admit, I was a little worried that Mr. Fogg’s passport wouldn’t have enough pages for all the countries we were going to visit.

66
00:05:50.226 --> 00:05:54.551
If he did run out, perhaps the visas could be stamped onto his forehead.

67
00:05:54.552 --> 00:06:00.718
Ha-ha! Don’t tell Mr. Fogg I said that!

